  {# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1 Einleitung ---- #}
  <section class="chapter-panel prose" id="voltage-window" data-chapter-panel aria-labelledby="t1">
    <h2 id="t1">1 · Follow the lithium: the voltage window</h2>

    <p>If you've ever tried to split a cell OCV into anode and cathode, you know the feeling: dozens of solutions that all sort of fit. That's not the battery's fault, it's the direction. We take the cell apart backwards. So it's worth flipping the question: how does a battery actually know how full its anode and cathode are?</p>
    <p>Look at it forwards and things get a lot simpler. And once you've seen it, you have guardrails for when you do have to take a cell apart backwards again.</p>
    <p>One caveat up front: we stay in equilibrium. So we're asking how the cell is built and where it ends up if you give it time. Kinetics and hysteresis stay out.</p>
    {{ note(draft, "Versprechen-Satz („guardrails…“) ggf. noch schärfen.") }}

    <h3>Step 1: Where does the lithium in a full cell come from?</h3>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz="lithium-source">
      <p class="quiz__question">Before you read on: where does the lithium come from?</p>
      <div class="quiz__options">
        <button type="button" data-answer="anode" data-correct="false" data-no="Not quite. The anode is built empty.">Anode</button>
        <button type="button" data-answer="cathode" data-correct="true" data-yes="Right. The cathode brings all of it.">Cathode</button>
        <button type="button" data-answer="electrolyte" data-correct="false" data-no="That's the obvious answer, but no. Read on.">Electrolyte</button>
      </div>
      <p class="quiz__feedback" data-quiz-feedback role="status" hidden></p>
    </div>
    <p>If you had to think about that for a second, this section is for you.</p>
    <p>The obvious answer is the electrolyte, there's lithium in there after all. But no. The conducting salt is only a means of transport. It doesn't change the capacity, no matter how much of it you pour in. The lithium comes out of the factory inside the cathode. So the cell is built discharged: cathode full, anode empty. Strictly speaking the cathode is even overfull, but we'll get to that with the N/Li ratio.</p>

    <h3>Step 2: One tank</h3>
    <p>From here on we only keep an eye on the lithium, because it's the only thing in the cell that really moves. Think of the lithium as water and the cathode as a tank. Water flows downhill. So does lithium. In equilibrium that's not a cute analogy, it's the actual physics.</p>
    <p>But what's up and what's down? We need something like sea level, a zero line to measure heights against. Only our zero hangs at the top: a ceiling, and we measure downwards from it. That ceiling is the Li/Li⁺ reference potential, and the voltage is the distance from the ceiling down to the water surface.</p>
    <p>By the way, one tank against the ceiling is exactly what you measure in the lab as a half cell against lithium metal.</p>
    {{ fig("intro", "tanks", "One tank against the ceiling: the voltage is the distance from the ceiling down to the water surface.", 'data-only="cathode"') }}

    <h3>Step 3: The second tank</h3>
    <p>If you just let the cathode drain, that would be a one-way trip. And where would the lithium go when you charge? You need a second tank: the anode. It hangs from the same ceiling but sits just below it, so low voltage. The cathode sits way down, so high voltage. Lithium, cathode, anode: that's all you need for equilibrium.</p>
    <p>And the cell voltage you measure from the outside? That's simply the height difference between the two water surfaces.</p>
    <p><strong>Charging</strong> means pumping water from the deep cathode tank up into the anode tank. That costs energy. <strong>Discharging</strong> means the water runs back down on its own, and the energy comes back as current.</p>
    {{ fig("intro", "tanks,electrodes", "Two tanks on one ceiling, charged: most of the water sits up in the anode, and the height difference is large. Right: the same state as two curves.", 'data-soc="0.85"') }}

    <h3>Step 4: Both tanks in one plot</h3>
    <p>Now both curves go into one plot. As the anode fills, its level moves toward the ceiling and its voltage drops, just like in any OCV curve on a datasheet. The cell voltage is the vertical distance between the two curves. For ideal tanks that's a straight line again.</p>
    {{ fig("intro", "electrodes,cell", "Top: both electrodes against the ceiling. Bottom: their distance is the cell voltage. Vmin and Vmax cut out the usable window (shaded).") }}
    <p>Now Vmin and Vmax come in: two horizontal lines. Where they cut the cell voltage, that's your usable capacity.</p>

