    <h3>Step 5: The real cell</h3>
    <p>The same thing with real curves, NMC against graphite, immediately looks wilder. But the balance stays the same. All the complexity sits in the shape of the material curves, not in the logic behind them.</p>
    {{ fig("intro", "tanks,electrodes", "The same balance with graphite against NMC. Graphite is a wide, flat tank with steps just below the ceiling, NMC a bulgy funnel. The bookkeeping doesn't change. Switch the tool above to real curves to play with it.", 'data-curves="real" data-vmin="2.8" data-vmax="4.2" data-soc="0.5"') }}
    <p>So far we've looked at the perfect picture: everything goes in, everything comes back out. Small spoiler: it's not quite that simple. But if we just follow the lithium, it stays as simple as it can possibly be. And next up: the two tanks aren't simply the same size. They have to be designed against each other.</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Work</strong> = amount of water × height difference = charge × voltage.</li>
        <li><strong>Ideal tank:</strong> the potential is a straight line in the lithium content, slope = 1 / cross-section (here: voltage span of the tank divided by its capacity).</li>
        <li><strong>Cell voltage</strong> = U<sub>cathode</sub>(x) − U<sub>anode</sub>(x), with x the lithium in the anode. Two straight lines, so their difference is a straight line too.</li>
        <li><strong>Usable capacity:</strong> solve U<sub>cell</sub>(x) = Vmin and U<sub>cell</sub>(x) = Vmax for x. Capacity = x<sub>Vmax</sub> − x<sub>Vmin</sub>.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <p class="chapter-next"><a href="#np-ratio">Next: the N/P ratio →</a></p>
  </section>

  {# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2 N/P ---- #}
  <section class="chapter-panel prose" id="np-ratio" data-chapter-panel aria-labelledby="t2">
    <h2 id="t2">2 · Sizing the tanks: the N/P ratio</h2>

    <p>So far we've simply assumed everything fits: the cathode gives up its lithium and the anode takes it in. But that doesn't happen by itself. The cathode brings all the lithium, so it sets how much water is in play. How big the anode tank has to be so that all of it fits is something you have to actively design.</p>
    <p>What happens if the anode tank is too small? During charging the level rises, and at some point it hits the ceiling. Then the anode sits at zero volts against lithium, and the lithium no longer goes into the anode but deposits as metal on its surface. That's called lithium plating. And this is where the ceiling pays off: it <em>is</em> lithium metal. So the overflowing water ends up right there, and the lithium becomes part of the ceiling itself. That's also why the anode can't drop below zero volts: its level is stuck to the ceiling. And it costs you capacity, because part of the plated lithium reacts with the electrolyte or loses contact and never comes back.</p>
    {{ fig("np", "tanks,electrodes", "Anode too small (N/P = 0.8): the anode is full before the cell reaches Vmax. What doesn't fit plates onto the ceiling, and the anode curve sits flat on zero.", 'data-np="0.8"') }}
    <p>And if the anode tank is too big? Then it never fills up. Empty space stays at the top, which you build, pay for and carry around without ever using it. And that's not even all: a bigger anode also costs more lithium during formation, but that's for the next chapter.</p>
    {{ fig("np", "tanks,electrodes", "Anode too big (N/P = 1.5): at Vmax a good part of the anode tank is still empty, and its curve stops well above the ceiling.", 'data-np="1.5"') }}
    <p>That's why the anode is designed a bit bigger, typically by around 10 %. For one, no coating is equally thick everywhere. For another, a real cell isn't in equilibrium when you charge it fast. Those 10 % are your safety distance to the ceiling.</p>

    <section class="ch-tool" data-chapter-tool="np" aria-label="Tool: N/P ratio">
