    {{ note(draft, "Ideale Tanks hier: Kathode 3,6–4,3 V, Anode 0,6–0,08 V. So gewählt, dass N/P knapp unter 1 bei Vmax 4,2 V die Decke erreicht. Bei N/P = 1,1 begrenzt oben die leere Kathode, nicht Vmax.") }}

    <p>Try it: push the N/P ratio below 1 and watch the anode reach the ceiling before the cell gets to Vmax. Push it well above 1, and you see how much of the anode tank stays empty.</p>
    <p>With real curves, NMC against graphite, exactly the same applies. Only now you see how tight it is: fully charged graphite sits only about 0.1 V below the ceiling. There isn't much room up there.</p>
    {{ fig("np", "tanks,electrodes", "Graphite against NMC at Vmax with N/P = 1.1: the anode curve runs flat, just above the ceiling.", 'data-curves="real" data-vmin="2.8"') }}
    <p>By the way, there are two definitions of N/P. One just compares the tank sizes. The other asks: how much room does the anode have left to the ceiling at Vmax? The second one is the more honest one for plating. And it depends on a quantity we've ignored so far: how much lithium actually takes part. That's what the next chapter is about.</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>N/P<sub>cap</sub></strong> = Q<sub>anode</sub> / Q<sub>cathode</sub>: ratio of the tank sizes, from half-cell capacities.</li>
        <li><strong>N/P<sub>fill</sub></strong> = Q<sub>anode</sub> / lithium in the anode at Vmax = 1 / anode fill at Vmax: how much room is left to the ceiling.</li>
        <li>These are two different definitions, and how they relate depends strongly on Vmax and Vmin. You can only convert one into the other in the linear case. With real curves it gets trickier and needs a clever algorithm.</li>
        <li>Each limit is one intersection of two straight lines. Play through the cases: cathode empty, anode not quite full, anode at the ceiling, Vmax reached. Who limits first?</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    {{ note(draft, "Namen N/P<sub>cap</sub> und N/P<sub>fill</sub> noch bestätigen.") }}
