  {# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3 N/Li ---- #}
  <section class="chapter-panel prose" id="n-li-ratio" data-chapter-panel aria-labelledby="t3">
    <h2 id="t3">3 · How much water is really in there: the N/Li ratio</h2>

    <p>So far we've assumed that all the water the cathode brings along actually takes part. It doesn't. Already during the very first charge, part of it goes missing, and the two tanks shift against each other. N/P tells you how big the tanks are. N/Li tells you how much water is really in there.</p>

    <h3>The cathode is overfull</h3>
    <p>For this, a quick look inside the cathode. Picture it as a mine, and the lithium as the support beams. So lithium isn't just charge, it's part of the structure. Pull the beams out during the first charge and the structure sags a little in places, and on discharge not everything fits back in.</p>
    <p>That sounds like a loss, but it isn't. The cathode simply leaves the factory overfull: it brings more lithium than it can take back afterwards. In the tank picture: on the first charge, more water flows over than fits back into the cathode later. So what happens to the surplus?</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "tanks", "Before the SEI takes its cut: the cathode brings 6 % more water than fits back in. With nothing withdrawn, the anode can't fully empty on discharge.", 'data-sei="0"') }}
    {{ note(draft, "Bergwerk: Passt „sackt zusammen“ zu „die Kathode verliert keine Kapazität“? Aufgelöst über „klingt nach Verlust, ist es aber nicht“.") }}

    <h3>The SEI takes its cut</h3>
    <p>The anode grabs it. During the first charge a thin layer forms on the anode, the SEI. The anode needs it to stay stable in the electrolyte at such low potentials at all. And that layer is built from lithium and electrolyte. The lithium in it is gone, for good. The bigger the anode, the more SEI.</p>
    <figure class="sei-figure">
