    </figure>
    {{ note(draft, "Platzhalter-Schema, von Claude gezeichnet. Bitte inhaltlich prüfen oder ersetzen.") }}

    <h3>Let's do the books</h3>
    <p>On the account sits what the cathode can take back, plus the bonus it brings from the factory by being overfull. The SEI makes a one-off withdrawal, and that's the real loss. The bonus covers part of it, but practically never all of it. So in the end you're short of lithium, not of room.</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "account", "The lithium account: reversible cathode capacity plus the formation bonus, minus the SEI. The dashed line is the room in the cathode.") }}
    <p>And how do you know how much gets withdrawn? You can measure it: build anode and cathode each against lithium metal, so against the ceiling, and see how much doesn't come back in the first cycle. For the cathode, this looks like a formation loss in the half cell. In the full cell, though, it isn't a loss but formation lithium, exactly the bonus that covers part of the SEI withdrawal. It just practically never covers all of it.</p>

    <h3>What this does to the plot{{ draftTag(draft) }}</h3>
    <p>In the plot, the two lines shift against each other. On discharge the anode runs empty before the cathode is full again, and a gap stays in the cathode. On charge the anode doesn't get as close to the ceiling anymore. The usable capacity is no longer set by the size of the tanks but by the lithium that's still in play.</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "tanks", "Discharged after formation: the anode is empty, and a gap stays in the cathode.") }}
    <p>And this closes the loop to the last chapter: how much room the anode has left to the ceiling at Vmax doesn't just depend on the tank size, but on the lithium. That's exactly why the second N/P definition is the more honest one.</p>

