    <h3>The real cell{{ draftTag(draft) }}</h3>
    <p>With NMC against graphite, the same shift immediately looks crooked: the graphite steps land in a different place relative to the NMC curve, and the cell curve changes shape. That's exactly why people despair when they fit stoichiometry windows, even though underneath it's just a shift.</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "electrodes,cell", "Graphite against NMC after formation, same lithium account as above.", 'data-curves="real" data-vmin="2.8"') }}
    {{ note(draft, "Echte Kurven: qualitativ lassen oder mit Zahlen?") }}

    <h3>Outlook: prelithiation{{ draftTag(draft) }}</h3>
    <p>If you're always short of lithium in the end, one idea is obvious: pay something into the account up front. That's exactly what prelithiation is. You give the cell extra lithium that covers the SEI withdrawal, so more of the cathode's lithium is left for cycling. But careful: more water also means the anode moves closer to the ceiling again on charge. N/P still looks relaxed, and the cell can plate anyway.</p>
    {{ note(draft, "Plating-Warnung überschneidet sich fast wörtlich mit dem Cliffhanger aus OCV-Artikel Teil 1.") }}

    <p>And if the SEI just keeps withdrawing after formation, cycle after cycle? Then N/Li keeps shifting. Welcome to aging.{{ draftTag(draft) }}</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it{{ draftTag(draft) }}</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Starting balance</strong> = first charge of the cathode half cell = Q<sub>cathode,rev</sub> + bonus.</li>
        <li><strong>Bonus</strong> = first charge − Q<sub>cathode,rev</sub> (from the first cycle of the cathode half cell).</li>
        <li><strong>SEI</strong> = first-cycle loss of the anode half cell, scales with the anode size.</li>
        <li><strong>Cyclable lithium</strong> Li<sub>cyc</sub> = starting balance − SEI. Lithium limits as long as SEI &gt; bonus, which is practically always.</li>
        <li><strong>N/Li</strong> = Q<sub>anode</sub> / Li<sub>cyc</sub>.</li>
        <li><strong>Ideal tanks:</strong> the anode line shifts against the cathode line by the missing lithium Q<sub>cathode,rev</sub> − Li<sub>cyc</sub>, which is exactly the gap in the cathode at Vmin.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
